<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diocese of Turku</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Archdiocese of Turku</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turku">Turku</a> is the oldest diocese in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, established in the 13th century, with its seat at Turku from about 1300. Under Swedish rule it covered all of Finland, and its cathedral and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, among them the reformer Mikael <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-julius-agricola">Agricola</a>, shaped the country's church and written language. Lutheran since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, it became the archdiocese of the Evangelical Lutheran Church of Finland in 1817.</p></div>
